{"result": {"response": {"id": "chatcmpl-EIXGN5QHxvPLSFbYayhrGqAJZxIZj", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"EDR Evasion, PPID Spoofing, and Anti-EDR Techniques\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) and the Windows telemetry, process structures, APIs, and detection mechanisms it uses. EDR connects to CreateProcessW, ETW-TI, kernel process structures, syscall hooks, process creation events, SIEM, SOAR, and EPP. A substantial linked subcommunity describes techniques intended to weaken or evade those controls, including PPID spoofing, command-line spoofing, suspended process creation, PEB overwrites, memory modification, call-stack spoofing, memory encryption, and EDR blinding. The community also includes dual-use research and adversary-simulation tooling such as SUPERMEGA, Cobalt Strike, Sliver, and Red Team activity, as well as books and technical publications documenting EDR operation and evasion. The data supports significant defensive and offensive-security relevance, but does not establish that any named entity was used in a real-world incident.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"EDR is the central defensive and analytical entity\",\"explanation\":\"EDR is the highest-connectivity entity in the community and is described as software that continuously monitors endpoint operating-system, user-mode, kernel, process, thread, memory, and other activity. It enriches telemetry with process details, command-line arguments, parent and initiating process information, and may inspect executable files or process memory. EDR then applies behavioral rules and event correlation to identify suspicious activity and produce alerts for analysts. [Data: Entities (7); Relationships (481, 275, 126, 127, 128)]\\n\\nThe community therefore represents an ecosystem organized around the visibility and trust assumptions of endpoint monitoring. EDR’s alerts can be forwarded to SIEM and SOAR infrastructure for investigation and response, while EPP is presented as a related technology focused more on interrupting or preventing attacks. [Data: Entities (139, 140, 138); Relationships (126, 127, 128)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Process creation telemetry is a primary detection surface\",\"explanation\":\"The data links EDR to CreateProcessW and to process-creation events, which can contain the originating process, child process, image filename, command line, and creation status. EDR can use this information to compare the process that initiated creation, the PPID supplied during the call, and the parent identifier recorded for the resulting child. [Data: Entities (422, 423, 279, 435); Relationships (481, 491, 504, 503)]\\n\\nThis makes process creation a key point where benign and malicious execution behavior may be distinguished. The community also connects process creation with STARTUPINFOEX and extended process attributes, showing that Windows process-launch APIs are both important sources of telemetry and mechanisms that can be manipulated. [Data: Entities (426, 430, 431); Relationships (487, 499, 500)]\"},{\"summary\":\"PPID spoofing threatens the reliability of process lineage\",\"explanation\":\"PPID spoofing is described as creating a child process with a fabricated parent-process identifier by setting the PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS attribute in a STARTUPINFOEX structure. The resulting child can appear to have been launched by a different parent than the process that actually initiated creation. [Data: Entities (421, 426, 427); Relationships (487, 499, 500)]\\n\\nThe technique is directly connected to EDR’s parent-process comparison logic and to post-creation process-parent queries. The data notes that user-space manipulation can make the kernel-stored inherited parent ID potentially untrustworthy, creating an important challenge for detections that rely on apparent process ancestry. [Data: Entities (432, 436, 413, 414); Relationships (501, 508, 503)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Command-line spoofing exploits differences between initial and later process state\",\"explanation\":\"Command-line spoofing involves manipulating or disguising command-line arguments, including values stored in the Process Environment Block (PEB), so that security tools may see misleading process information. The PEB contains process parameters such as the executable image name and command line, but the data explicitly states that the owning process can modify the PEB and that its contents should not be treated as inherently trustworthy. [Data: Entities (385, 122, 363, 425); Relationships (408, 485)]\\n\\nThe community connects this technique with suspended process creation and a time-of-check-to-time-of-use weakness. A suspended child can have its arguments, PEB, memory, or other attributes modified before execution resumes, while EDR may compare the arguments captured at process creation with later values queried from the child. [Data: Entities (424, 384, 423); Relationships (484, 476, 486)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community includes multiple anti-EDR evasion approaches\",\"explanation\":\"Several named projects and techniques are linked to EDR as approaches for concealing or altering execution visibility. These include memory-encryption or stealth-execution projects such as SWAPPALA / SLE(A)PING, Gargoyle, Cronos, and Foliage; thread-pool execution represented by Thread Pool / Pool Party; and call-stack manipulation or masking projects including ThreadStackSpoofer, CallStackSpoofer, AceLdr, CallStackMasker, Unwinder, and TitanLdr. [Data: Relationships (129, 130, 131, 133, 134, +more); Entities (141, 142, 143, 145, 146, +more)]\\n\\nEDR blinding is separately described as reducing or losing visibility by modifying ntdll.dll hooks on which a security product depends. The broader EDR-attacks entity connects these approaches to exploitation of sensor models, event trust levels, kernel-access requirements, process-memory visibility, and time-of-check-to-time-of-use weaknesses. [Data: Entities (462, 383, 482); Relationships (559, 429, 593)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Memory and image-loading telemetry provide complementary detection context\",\"explanation\":\"The data describes runtime memory modification as changes to data inside a process that may or may not generate telemetry depending on the mechanism used. A byte-wise memory-copy loop is specifically linked to runtime memory modification and is described as potentially avoiding telemetry associated with a hooked RtlCopyMemory call. [Data: Entities (234, 235); Relationships (268, 266)]\\n\\nEDR also receives operating-system telemetry through ETW, ETW-TI, kernel callbacks, and the Microsoft-Windows-kernel-Process provider. The ImageLoad event can record information such as process identifiers, image path, base address, checksum, and image size; SilkETW is linked to recording such events, including an example involving Notepad. [Data: Entities (223, 221, 222, 308, 312, +more); Relationships (241, 251, 252, 329, 330)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The material is dual-use, spanning research, red teaming, and offensive tooling\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes SUPERMEGA, described as a publicly available loader laboratory and code project for experimenting with executable loading and shellcode injection, and Cordyceps EXE Injection, which is linked as a companion project or article in the same series. [Data: Entities (93, 153); Relationships (141, 143)]\\n\\nIt also includes Cobalt Strike and Sliver as example beacon or shellcode payloads that a loader may attempt to execute without triggering EDR detection, and connects these tools to Red Team operators seeking to avoid endpoint-security alerts. The evidence supports offensive-security and adversary-simulation relevance, but it does not by itself demonstrate malicious use. [Data: Entities (161, 162, 166); Relationships (152, 153, 159)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Technical publications form a knowledge and dissemination layer\",\"explanation\":\"The community contains the Anti-EDR Compendium and the “How EDR Works” article, both linked to EDR and focused on telemetry, detection behavior, alerts, and evasion concepts. The compendium is hosted on Deeb.ch. [Data: Entities (171, 164, 165); Relationships (155, 157, 156)]\\n\\nAdditional cited resources include Matt Hand’s “Evading EDR,” Kyle Cucci’s “Evasive Malware,” Amazon’s listing for Hand’s book, and No Starch Press’s association with Cucci’s publication. These relationships indicate that the community is supported by written technical material covering both EDR operation and offensive or evasive malware techniques. [Data: Entities (155, 158, 154, 157, 167, +more); Relationships (145, 149, 144, 148, 160, +more)]\"}],\"rating\":8.5,\"rating_explanation\":\"The rating is high because the community combines central endpoint-defense infrastructure with multiple technically specific methods for manipulating process lineage, memory, telemetry, and execution visibility, although the data does not establish actual malicious deployment.\"}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1788086475, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1995, "prompt_tokens": 7809, "total_tokens": 9804, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 102, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 7806, "cached_tokens": 0}}, "formatted_response": null, "content": "{\"title\":\"EDR Evasion, PPID Spoofing, and Anti-EDR Techniques\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) and the Windows telemetry, process structures, APIs, and detection